  "ROOM_COURT_MOTTO": "SERVICE IS NOT ACQUITTAL",
  "CASE_MATVEY_NAME": "Matvey",
  "CASE_MATVEY_ROLE": "Granary owner · The price of salvation",
  "CASE_MATVEY_FILE": "CIRCUMSTANCES\nDuring a crop failure, Matvey quadrupled the price of his grain. The first proceeds paid for his daughter's treatment. He turned away families unable to pay in full.\n\nINVENTORY\nStocks were sufficient for sales at the old price and a winter reserve. Matvey signed the inventory before raising prices.\n\nORDER\n“No credit for Anna. Keep the sacks for paying customers.” Anna was asking for grain for her children.\n\nLEDGER\nThe price remained high after treatment was fully paid for. Further proceeds stayed in his personal account.\n\nThe grain belonged to Matvey; no one had entrusted their stocks to him.",
  "CASE_MATVEY_INTRO": "I should not be here. I saved my daughter!",
  "CASE_MATVEY_DIALOGUE_1_QUESTION": "Why did you have to raise the price?",
  "CASE_MATVEY_DIALOGUE_1_ANSWER": "The treatment needed money at once. I decided to save her first and worry about everyone else later.",
  "CASE_MATVEY_DIALOGUE_2_QUESTION": "But treatment was paid for. Why keep the price?",
  "CASE_MATVEY_DIALOGUE_2_ANSWER": "I feared another illness. Then poverty. The money kept coming, and I still thought: not enough.",
  "CASE_MATVEY_DIALOGUE_3_QUESTION": "Why refuse Anna when there was enough grain?",
  "CASE_MATVEY_DIALOGUE_3_ANSWER": "She offered to work for it. But a man with money stood behind her. I was already counting what I'd lose by choosing her.",
  "CASE_MATVEY_ARCHIVE": "Anna at the granary: “Let the children eat. I'll work for it.” She once sewed clothes for his daughter. Matvey looks at the sacks and closes the ledger: “Cash only.” The next customer is already reaching for his purse.",
  "CASE_MATVEY_FACTS_1": "Turning away the needy for greater profit",
  "CASE_MATVEY_FACTS_2": "The grain price was higher than before",
  "CASE_MATVEY_FACTS_3": "He paid for his daughter's treatment",
  "CASE_MATVEY_EXPLANATION": "Circle IV — greed. Matvey knew supplies were sufficient, yet refused the needy for greater returns. His daughter's treatment explains the first sale, not the continued profiteering. Price alone is insufficient: the defining fact is his deliberate choice of profit.",
  "CASE_AGATA_NAME": "Agatha",
  "CASE_AGATA_ROLE": "Seller of “healing water” · A borrowed signature",
  "CASE_AGATA_FILE": "CIRCUMSTANCES\nAgatha sold ordinary water as medicine, advertising it in a doctor's name.\n\nADVERTISING LEAFLET\n“I have tested the formula. This remedy helps with severe fever. Dr. Orlov.”\n\nORLOV'S DENIAL\n“I did not test the remedy or authorize the use of my name.”\n\nNOTE TO AN ASSISTANT\nAgatha: “The bottles contain ordinary water. I'll dictate the endorsement. Sign Orlov's name yourself.”\n\nMARIA'S TESTIMONY\nTrusting the doctor's signature, she bought water for her son with money set aside for an examination. She had to postpone seeking medical care.",
  "CASE_AGATA_INTRO": "There has been a mistake. I helped people keep their hope. Do you think I'm a fraud?",
  "CASE_AGATA_DIALOGUE_1_QUESTION": "What did you promise your customers?",
  "CASE_AGATA_DIALOGUE_1_ANSWER": "That the water would help the sick. People stopped crying. I wanted to believe that was help, too.",
  "CASE_AGATA_DIALOGUE_2_QUESTION": "Orlov denies endorsing it. Who wrote it?",
  "CASE_AGATA_DIALOGUE_2_ANSWER": "I dictated it. My assistant wrote it down and signed. Orlov had no part in it. His name was meant to convince buyers.",
  "CASE_AGATA_DIALOGUE_3_QUESTION": "Why not say it was ordinary water?",
  "CASE_AGATA_DIALOGUE_3_ANSWER": "Then Maria would have walked past. I knew that before the sale. So I kept the borrowed signature.",
  "CASE_AGATA_ARCHIVE": "The assistant suggests an honest label: “Ordinary water. No proven medicinal effect.” Agatha hands back the advertisement: “Nobody will buy it that way. Keep the endorsement.” He silently takes up his pen.",
  "CASE_AGATA_FACTS_1": "She charged money for bottles of water",
  "CASE_AGATA_FACTS_2": "False medicine and a forged endorsement",
  "CASE_AGATA_FACTS_3": "The buyer wanted to believe it would help",
  "CASE_AGATA_EXPLANATION": "Circle VIII — fraud. Agatha knowingly sold water as medicine under a false medical endorsement. Profit accompanied the act, but deception enabled the sale. Maria's hope did not mean consent to be deceived.",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_NAME": "Severin",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_ROLE": "Shelter keeper · A place in the boat",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_FILE": "CIRCUMSTANCES\nSeverin hid families from raids, then sold their pursuers a map of the entrance.\n\nPROMISE TO THE FAMILIES\n“The address stays between us.” Signed by Severin.\n\nRECEIPT\n“Map delivered. Payment received. Informed that everyone inside will be taken.” The signature matches the promise.\n\nRAID RECORD\nThe pursuers used this map to enter the shelter. The families were arrested and the building burned.\n\nTICKET\nHis brother had already paid for Severin's place in a boat. He could leave without selling the address, but could not take everyone with him.",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_INTRO": "I don't belong here. I hid people from the raids. Without me, they would have been caught sooner.",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_DIALOGUE_1_QUESTION": "Why did you decide to leave the shelter?",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_DIALOGUE_1_ANSWER": "Every night I waited for a knock at the door. There was one place in the boat. I wanted to live, too.",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_DIALOGUE_2_QUESTION": "You already had a ticket. Why sell the address?",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_DIALOGUE_2_ANSWER": "To have something to start with on the other shore. I told myself they would be found anyway.",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_DIALOGUE_3_QUESTION": "Did the families know you handed over the map?",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_DIALOGUE_3_ANSWER": "No. They were waiting for me to return. And I took the money, knowing someone would come for them.",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_ARCHIVE": "Severin slips the map inside his coat. A girl asks: “Shall I leave the light on until you come back?” He nods and leaves. A lit window remains behind him.",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_FACTS_1": "Payment for handing over the map",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_FACTS_2": "He feared for his own life",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_FACTS_3": "Betraying people he had taken under protection",
  "CASE_SEVERIN_EXPLANATION": "Circle IX — treachery. Severin betrayed people who had entrusted him with their safety. He is judged for selling their secret, not for fear or departure. Money was the motive; the defining circumstance was breaking his promise of protection.",
  "CIRCLE_1": "I · Limbo",
  "RULE_1": "Insufficient evidence for another circle.",
  "CIRCLE_2": "II · Lust",
  "RULE_2": "Harm in pursuit of possessing a person or satisfying desire.",
  "CIRCLE_3": "III · Gluttony",
  "RULE_3": "Excessive consumption that deprives others of necessities.",
  "CIRCLE_4": "IV · Greed",
  "RULE_4": "Deliberate harm for profit and accumulation.",
  "CIRCLE_5": "V · Wrath",
  "RULE_5": "Harm driven by revenge or hatred.",
  "CIRCLE_6": "VI · Heresy",
  "RULE_6": "Forcibly imposing dogma despite known harm.",
  "CIRCLE_7": "VII · Violence",
  "RULE_7": "Intentional severe violence. Necessary defense is an exception.",
  "CIRCLE_8": "VIII · Fraud",
  "RULE_8": "Deception as the principal means of exploiting others.",
  "CIRCLE_9": "IX · Treachery",
  "RULE_9": "Grave harm through a deliberate breach of special trust."
}

